you  hi everyone it's Jerry this is video 18  from the beginner to chess master  playlist which is a progressive series  of videos and I have a quick little end  game technique to share with you this is  a technique I'm revisiting specifically  for this playlist you may recall it from  a couple years back I have exactly three  examples to share with you in this  position it's white to move and the big  question is can the White King catch the  black pawn will he be there in time  let's say so I'll throw it to you if  you'd like to go ahead pause the video  will the white king be able to track  that pawn down okay the answer is yes  and we can see that by mapping out the  moves  sure enough the white King will be there  in time to successfully track the pond  down there are a couple approaches we  could have taken to figure this out  one is to count one two three four five  one two three four five white would be  on move at that point going from G to  and then making the capture on the h1  square so we could have counted we could  have maybe visualized  both pieces moving or maybe you know  some combination of the two I don't use  either approach I don't count  I don't visualize so what approach do I  take well whether it's a slower game or  a faster game this is the approach or  this is the technique that I use when is  the Kings turn to move what I do is  envision that just for a moment the pawn  is a bishop and I draw a diagonal down  towards the promotion rank so this is  the diagonal that I'm referring to and  this is the diagonal of the following  square or box now this box is  significant because if I could answer  the simple question can the King enter  the box and by enter the box I mean to  be anywhere on the edge or inside of the  box if the King could enter the box and  he can well he can now  catch the pond if the king could enter  the box he will be able to catch the  pond so we're notice we're not counting  we're not visualizing both pieces moving  we're just doing a little artwork  drawing in a diagonal and the box  connected with it if the king could  enter the box he can catch the pawn it's  as simple as that let's have a look at  just our second of three examples once  again white to move go ahead and use  that technique and see if you can  quickly answer this one can the King  catch the pawn okay the answer to this  is no and keep in mind we go ahead and  draw in that diagonal in the Box  connected with it when it is the Kings  turn to move so it is the King's turn to  move here this is the diagonal this is  the box connected with it can the King  edge of the box  unfortunately no also bear in mind that  whenever that pawn makes a move we can  revisit that same question in this  position it's White's move  the new box here is this one can the  King enter that box it's getting smaller  once again he's not able to enter it he  will not be there in time to track the  pond down let's have a look at just one  more example this one right here where  it is now black to move if you like to  go ahead pause the video will the white  king be able to catch the black pawn ok  the answer to this is no keep in mind we  go ahead and draw in that box when it is  the King's turn to move in this position  it's black on move after h5 now we go  ahead and draw the diagonal and the box  connected with it can the King enter the  box unfortunately he cannot therefore he  will not be able to successfully track  down that pawn in time so as mentioned  this is something that I use whether it  be a slower game or a faster game it's a  very useful technique it'll save us at  least just a little bit on some of our  calculation workload just a nice little  trick to be familiar with so that's all  I have for you in this one as usual I  hope you got something out of it and  I'll catch you in the next one that's  all for now take care bye  you

